// Hold the NVIDIA driver stack, and the kernel it is built for, out of
// unattended-upgrades.
//
// Why: when unattended-upgrades bumps the NVIDIA stack (driver + userspace +
// kernel module) in the background, the *running* kernel module stays at the
// old version while the installed userspace jumps ahead, until the next
// reboot ("NVRM: API mismatch" in dmesg). On a machine whose dGPU drives the
// panel, rebooting out of that half-upgraded state can wedge in the GPU
// shutdown path and never reset, needing a hard power-off. Holding these
// packages means the driver only ever moves during a deliberate `apt upgrade`
// that the user follows with a reboot.
//
// Kernels: the prebuilt NVIDIA module is a separate package per kernel, so
// holding the stack alone lets unattended-upgrades install a new kernel with
// no module for it, and the next boot puts the panel on the firmware
// framebuffer. The kernel metapackages and every per-kernel image and module
// package (the ZFS modules a ZFS root cannot boot without included) are held
// with it, so a kernel and its modules only ever move together.
//
// Scope: this affects only the automatic (unattended-upgrades) path.
// Interactive `apt upgrade` still updates NVIDIA normally. provision installs
// this only where the NVIDIA userspace driver is installed, since elsewhere
// the kernel entries would hold kernels back for nothing.
//
// Pattern: unattended-upgrades matches each blacklist entry against the
// package name ANCHORED AT THE START (re.match, and it builds an apt pin
// `/^<entry>/`). A bare "nvidia" therefore matches only names that BEGIN with
// nvidia and silently misses libnvidia-*, xserver-xorg-video-nvidia-*, and
// the linux-{modules,objects,signatures,firmware}-nvidia-* packages — the
// kernel module and GL/compute halves whose split causes the very hang above.
// The leading ".*" is load-bearing: keep it. Do NOT "simplify" to "nvidia".
// The kernel entries are plain prefixes for the same reason.
//
// Trade-off: background *security* updates to the NVIDIA stack and to the
// kernel are deferred until a manual upgrade. Accepted: a wedged reboot, or a
// kernel that boots without its GPU driver, is worse.
// Revert: delete this file.
// Full incident writeup: thinkpad-p16-gen3-ubuntu-suspend.md
Unattended-Upgrade::Package-Blacklist {
    ".*nvidia";
    "linux-generic";
    "linux-image-";
    "linux-modules-";
    "linux-main-modules-";
};
